      'The English teacher in a Cape Coast day school stopped collecting exercise books when she found the same village-journey composition in four different handwritings. The uncle, the broken-down lorry, and the moral at the end were identical. When she asked the writers to retell the story without looking, two of them stalled after the first sentence. They had stored a past-paper answer, not a writing skill.',
      'WAEC English Language is built around unseen work. You will meet a passage you have not revised, a summary you cannot copy, and a composition prompt that will not match last year\'s letter about a leaking hostel. Cramming old full answers trains you to hunt for a familiar plot. The paper rewards what you can do with a new task in a fixed time.',
      'Treat comprehension as a weekday habit. Take one short passage from an old paper or a newspaper comment page. After one careful reading, write two sentences that capture the writer\'s main point. Then write a third sentence that a careless reader might add but that the text never supports. Crossing out that third sentence is the real exercise: it teaches you to stay inside what is written.',
      'Summary is a different job from comprehension. Once you know what the passage is doing, list the points the question actually asks for. Turn each point into a short clause in your own words. Drop illustrations, names used only as colour, and repeated emphasis. Time the work: a quarter of an hour to read and select, ten minutes to write. If you always overrun, you are still transferring sentences instead of choosing them.',
      'For continuous writing, collect structures, not ready-made stories. A narrative needs a time order, a problem, and an ending that shows a change in the character or the situation. An argument needs a clear position, two or three reasons with examples from school or town life, and a closing that does not open a new debate. Practise the switch: write a story about a prefect election one evening, then argue the next evening whether prefects should be appointed.',
      'Keep an error book that records the faults your teacher actually circles. Subject-verb disagreement, tense drift in the middle of a story, run-on sentences, and a vague this with no noun after it lose marks quietly. Each week, take one old paragraph and rewrite it with those faults removed. New vocabulary sticks when you lift a useful verb from reading and use it in a sentence of your own the same day.',
      'Past papers still belong in the plan if you sit them as tests. In the last two months, do one full comprehension-and-summary sitting each week against a clock. Mark with a teacher or with a published scheme if your school has one. Track the question types that leak marks: words in context, inference, or a summary that ignores the word limit. Repair the type, not that particular passage.',
      'Essay drafts should be timed, left overnight, and read cold. Ask four questions. Did I answer the prompt as written? Are there several paragraphs with different jobs, or one slab of handwriting? Did I spend the first page on a proverb that never connects? Can a stranger see where the introduction ends and the first reason begins? A marker working at speed needs those joints to be visible.',
      'If your school still runs the oral paper, do not leave sound work until the test week. Read a paragraph aloud and tap the stressed syllable in longer words. With a classmate, dictate five words and write them down. Hearing and spelling support each other. Silent rereading of model answers does nothing for that paper.',
      'Cramming fails for a second, practical reason. The examination recycles skills, not plots. You may meet another letter of complaint, but it will not be about the same hostel roof. A memorised roof leaves you empty when the prompt is a noisy street. Purpose, audience, and paragraph jobs travel. A stored composition does not.',
      'A weekly rhythm that survives extra classes looks modest. Two comprehension passages. One summary. One full essay or letter. One session repairing errors from your notebook. One timed objective practice if that paper is on your timetable. Skip a session when you are unwell. Do not catch up by reading a stack of sample essays after midnight. Put the sessions on a real calendar with clock times, not as a wish list on the back of an exercise book.',
      'Well-meaning relatives may urge you to learn the likely questions. Listen, then keep your method. Rumours about topics are not a syllabus. If you can compress a page about farming, you can compress a page about traffic. If you can argue with examples from your market, you can argue from a prompt you have never seen.',
      'End each study block with one honest line about the skill that still fails: I invent facts in inference items, or my narratives stop without an ending. That line is tomorrow\'s first task. Cramming covers the hole. Named practice fills it.',
      'On the morning of the paper you will not need a memorised uncle or a memorised lorry. You will need a method you have already used on strange texts, in a hall that is too warm, with a passage that does not care what you revised. That is the only English preparation that still works when the question paper is opened.',
