      'WAEC Mathematics is a stack of skills that depend on each other. If number work is shaky, algebraic fractions collapse. If you cannot find a bearing, the trigonometry word problem becomes a drawing you abandon. A plan that hops randomly between topics feels busy and leaves the same holes. You need blocks, then mixed practice, then a week that only repairs what the mixed papers exposed.',
      'Week 1 belongs to number and numeration. Fractions, ratios, percentages, standard form, and number bases appear both as short items and as the arithmetic inside later questions. Sit a set of old objective items on these, then two structured questions. Write every line. If you reach for a calculator in a section that does not allow one, you have found a weakness, not a shortcut.',
      'Week 2 is algebraic processes: simplification, factorisation, equations, change of subject, and variation. Many candidates can expand a bracket and still cannot solve a worded equation because they never translate the sentence into symbols. Practise that translation with market and age problems from old papers. Mark the method, not only the final boxed number.',
      'Week 3 covers mensuration and plane geometry: areas, volumes, angles in polygons, and circle theorems if they are on your syllabus. Draw the figure every time, even when the paper already printed one. Label what you know. A blank diagram is how people lose the first two marks before they start calculating.',
      'Week 4 is statistics and probability: mean, median, mode, pie charts, histograms, and simple probability. Show the formula and the substitution. Examiners award process marks when the arithmetic slips but the steps are present. They cannot award them when only a lonely wrong total sits on the page.',
      'Week 5 is trigonometry, bearings, and, where your syllabus includes them, graphs of sine and cosine. Sketch the north line. State whether you are using sine, cosine, or tangent before you punch numbers. Wrong-ratio errors are common and expensive. Do at least one bearing question every other day this week so the diagram becomes automatic.',
      'Week 6 gathers sets, functions, and remaining algebra such as simultaneous equations and quadratic graphs. This is also the week to stop saying you do not do constructions. Book a long session with compass and ruler. Construction questions are slow if you have not touched the instruments since junior school. They are routine if you have.',
      'Week 7 is mixed timed papers. Sit one Paper 1 and one Paper 2 under exam timing, on separate days. Do not pause to look up a formula. After marking, list every question you skipped or guessed. Those titles become the only topics for Week 8. A mixed paper is a diagnosis. It is wasted if you only count the score and hide the script.',
      'Week 8 is repair, not a new syllabus. Re-teach yourself the two or three topics that leaked the most marks. Then sit the feared paper type again: the construction, the proof-style geometry, or the long statistics question you always left blank. Finishing an eight-week plan means you met the question you used to turn past, not that you highlighted a textbook from cover to cover.',
      'Daily timing matters as much as the weekly label. Forty-five focused minutes with a closed phone beat three hours of Maths with chat in another tab. Start with a warm-up of five short items, then one longer question, then mark immediately. Immediate marking is how you catch a repeated slip, such as forgetting to convert centimetres to metres.',
      'When you skip a topic because it scares you, you are training the exam hall to scare you in the same place. Put the feared question first twice a week, while you still have energy. If you cannot start it, write the first diagram and the first formula anyway. Starting is a skill. Blank pages earn nothing.',
      'Parents sometimes buy a thick past-question book and expect it to be read like a novel. Use it as a gym. One paper, marked, with a list of broken topics, is worth more than twenty papers you went through by glancing at printed solutions. Copying a printed working line by line feels like study. It is not the same as producing the working from a blank sheet.',
      'If a week collapses because of a funeral, a power cut, or extra lessons, do not try to double the next week. Keep the topic label and cut the quantity. Two good questions on circle theorems beat a panicked skim of four chapters. The plan is a finishable spine, not a punishment.',
      'By the last school day before the paper, you should know which formula lives in your head and which diagram you still draw too slowly. That knowledge is the point of eight weeks. A chart that only says Maths will not tell you. A chart that names number, algebra, shape, data, and the paper you used to skip will.',
