      'WAEC essay questions are jobs with a shape. Narrative, descriptive, argumentative, and article writing are not the same job in different clothes. If you treat every prompt as write until the pages look full, you produce atmosphere without a spine. Examiners can mark quickly when they can see the function of each paragraph. They stall when they have to search for your point.',
      'Before you write a line of the essay, spend five minutes on a plan that names paragraph jobs. For a story: setting and character, the incident, the complication, the turning action, the result. For an argument: your position, reason one with an example, reason two, a fair look at the other side if the question invites it, a close. For an article: audience, purpose, two or three developed points, a recommendation. The plan is not decoration. It is the map the marker will feel even if they never see it.',
      'The first paragraph has one job: show that you read the question. If the prompt asks for a story that ends with I had never been so ashamed, your opening should put a person in a place where shame could happen. If you spend that paragraph on a proverb about patience, you have not started. Markers notice when the first page could sit on any question from the last ten years.',
      'Each middle paragraph should do one piece of work and then stop. In a narrative, that might be the moment the lorry fails, then the choice to walk, then the meeting that changes the day. In an argument, one paragraph, one reason. Mixing three reasons in one block hides the second and third from a fast reader. A short, complete paragraph is easier to reward than a long, tangled one.',
      'Endings are where many decent scripts lose the last band of marks. A story needs an ending that matches the required last sentence or the implied change. An argument needs a close that restates the position without copying the introduction word for word. Do not add a new character in the final four lines. Do not announce In conclusion and then wander into a different topic.',
      'Language marks sit on top of this structure. Varied sentences help, but a string of forced idioms does not. One exact verb is worth more than three inflated phrases. Keep tenses stable in a story: if you start in the past, do not drift into the present for a page and back again. Check agreement when the subject is a long noun phrase. These are the quiet errors that turn a readable essay into a script that looks careless.',
      'Time is part of the structure. If the paper gives you fifty minutes for the essay, you cannot spend twenty on the plan and thirty on one huge first paragraph. Ten minutes to plan, thirty to write, ten to read aloud in your head and mend. Leaving ten minutes for that last read is how you catch the missing ending, the unanswered last sentence, and the paragraph that never got a full stop.',
      'Common unfinished mistakes are specific. You tell a story in which the conflict never arrives. You describe a place and forget the people. You argue both sides so evenly that you never take a position the question asked for. You write an article that sounds like a letter to a friend, with slang and no heading. You ignore a word limit or a required ending. Each of these is a planning failure, not a vocabulary failure.',
      'Practise with the exact instruction words. Write a story is not write an account of your last holiday unless the question says so. Write an article for publication in your school magazine needs a title and a tone that could be pinned on a notice board. Write a letter is a different paper job; do not import letter greetings into an essay. Train the eye to circle the genre before you invent content.',
      'A useful drill is to take one old prompt and write only the plan and the first and last paragraphs in fifteen minutes. Then, another day, fill the middle. This stops you from falling in love with an opening you cannot finish. Another drill: swap scripts with a classmate and ask them to label the job of each paragraph in the margin. If they cannot, the examiner will not either.',
      'Teachers sometimes praise a colourful opening and ignore a missing spine. Thank them for the encouragement, then ask a colder question: could someone mark this in three minutes and know what happened? If the answer is no, add the missing turn. Colour is optional. Completeness is not.',
      'On the exam day, write the plan on the question paper or in the margin if the instructions allow rough work. If you freeze, write the paragraph jobs as five short headings and fill them. A complete, plain essay with a beginning, a middle, and an end will always be easier to mark than a brilliant fragment that stops in the market with the yam still frying.',
      'When you reread, look for the unfinished feel: a character who vanished, a reason that was named and never illustrated, a required final sentence that was never used. Repair those before you hunt for a fancier adjective. Markers are not looking for a novel. They are looking for a job they can tick through without guessing what you meant to do.',
