      'Old WAEC papers are most valuable when they behave like a scan of your current skill. Timing, weak topics, and command words show up if you sit the paper under exam rules and then mark it. They stay hidden if you flip to the answer page as soon as you feel stuck. The goal is not to remember that a 2019 Paper 2 item was about a particular experiment. The goal is to learn how you fail when a clock is running.',
      'Start with timing. Sit one paper in the official duration, in one sitting, with the materials the rubric allows. If you used two hours for a paper that is one hour twenty, your score is not an exam score. Note where the time went: a long theory question, a set of objectives you second-guessed, or a blank you stared at. That map tells you what to drill next week.',
      'After marking, sort misses into three buckets. Bucket one: you did not know the content. Bucket two: you knew it but misread the command word. Bucket three: you knew it and ran out of time. These buckets need different repairs. Content misses go to a textbook and a worked example. Command-word misses go to a short drill where you rewrite the same facts as state, explain, and describe. Time misses go to mixed timed sets, not to more rereading.',
      'Command words are the examiner\'s instructions, not decoration. List and state want brief points. Explain wants a reason or a mechanism. Describe wants features or a sequence. Compare wants both sides. Calculate wants working. Suggest often wants a reasonable application, not a memorised definition. Keep a one-page list of these verbs in your book and tick which ones you regularly ignore.',
      'Use topic tallies. After three papers in the same subject, write the syllabus headings across a page and mark a tick for every question you lost. Clusters appear. In English, it might be summary. In Mathematics, constructions. In Biology, ecology calculations. Those clusters are your next two weeks. Random rereading of the whole syllabus feels responsible and wastes the information the papers already gave you.',
      'Do not memorise model essays or model calculations as answers to reuse. The numbers will change. The story prompt will change. What you may reuse is the method: how you set out a calculation, how you plan an essay, how you attach a unit, how you draw a table. Method is portable. A stored final answer is a trap.',
      'When you review a printed solution, hide it until you have written your own attempt, even a failed one. Then compare steps, not just the boxed result. Ask where your path left the marking scheme. Did you skip a definition the scheme always pays? Did you give an example when the question asked for a difference? That comparison is study. Copying the printed working onto your page is handwriting practice.',
      'A sensible rotation is one timed paper each week per heavy subject in the last two months, and shorter topic drills on the other days. Sitting seven full papers in a weekend teaches endurance and little else if none of them is marked. Marking is the part that turns the paper into a diagnosis.',
      'Beware of compiled likely-question booklets that reprint the same items with new covers. They can still be useful as extra drills if you treat them as drills. They are not a prophecy. Building a revision list from your own miss-tally is safer than trusting a cover that says the questions are the ones that must appear.',
      'Teachers can help most after you bring a marked paper, not before you have sat one. Ask them to look at the bucket of command-word misses. A ten-minute conversation about why outline is not discuss will save more marks than a two-hour unfocused extra class. Bring the script. Point at the verb.',
      'If you study in a group, do not use the group to share memorised answers. Use it to mark each other against the command word. Read a question aloud, hear three attempts, and decide which one actually did the verb. That is a legitimate use of past papers: they supply the verbs and the topics. Your group supplies the honesty.',
      'The last week before the exam is late to sit a paper you then refuse to mark. It is not late to sit one section, mark it the same evening, and repair one cluster. Diagnosis can be small. What does not help is rereading old solutions until they feel familiar and calling that familiarity readiness.',
      'Used this way, a past-question book earns its keep. It tells you how long you really take, which topics still leak, and which verbs you still skip. It will not sit the new paper for you. The new paper will still be new. Your advantage is that you have already watched yourself work under the same kind of pressure, and you have already repaired the failures that showed up on the page.',
