      'A biology marker paused on a drawing of a bean seed that had been shaded until it looked like a painting. The hilum was in the wrong place. The labels pointed into empty space. There was no title and no magnification. The candidate had spent minutes making the sketch dark and real. The scheme was looking for a clear outline, correct relative size of parts, and labels written horizontally with ruled guidelines. Pretty is optional. Accuracy is the mark.',
      'WAEC Biology diagrams are communication. The examiner must recognise the structure, read the labels without guessing, and see that you know which parts matter for that question. A small, smudged drawing in the corner of the page fails before the biology is even assessed. Size, line, and label position are part of the answer.',
      'Use a sharp pencil and a firm, single outline. Do not shade large areas. Do not use colours unless the paper asks for them. Hairy, sketched-over lines make it hard to see the intended shape. If you misdraw, put a single line through the figure and draw beside it. A second clean outline is better than a pad of grey graphite.',
      'Scale and proportion earn marks even when artistic talent does not. If the question is a transverse section, the relative thickness of layers should not be random. If you draw a flower, the ovary should not dwarf the petals unless that is true of the specimen. Look at the object or the printed diagram and copy relationships, not a memory of a cartoon from a phone.',
      'Labels need ruled guidelines that touch the part, do not cross each other, and end in a horizontal name. Write the biological term, not a description in brackets, unless the question asks you to annotate a function. Xylem is a label. Tubes that carry water may be an annotation if requested. Mixing the two can cost a precision mark.',
      'Give the drawing a title that names the view and the organism or organ. Longitudinal section of a maize grain is a title. My diagram is not. State the magnification if you worked from a specimen: measured drawing size over measured specimen size. If you worked from a printed figure, follow the paper\'s instruction; do not invent a magnification that you did not calculate.',
      'Drawing from a specimen is not the same as drawing from a textbook memory. If a flower is on the bench, draw that flower, including a damaged petal if it is there, rather than a perfect diagram you sketched in Form 2. Markers compare your figure with the specimen or with the printed figure in the question. A remembered textbook onion cell that does not match the slide in front of you will lose proportion marks even if it looks tidy. Trust the object. Then label what you actually drew.',
      'Draw only what the question asks. A full plant when the question wants a leaf epidermis wastes time and adds wrong labels. A cell with every organelle you can remember, when the question is about a palisade cell\'s chloroplasts, creates extra chances to mislabel. Economy is not laziness here. It is focus.',
      'Common failures are predictable. Arrowheads that miss the structure. Labels written vertically along the stem of the line. Unlabelled lines. A diagram so small that two labels sit on the same part. Using a pen that blobs. Copying a textbook three-dimensional style that you cannot control, instead of a simple section. Each of these is avoidable with a slow last minute of checking.',
      'Practise under time. A diagram question in a theory paper may give you little more than five to eight minutes. That is enough for an outline, five accurate labels, and a title if you do not shade. It is not enough if you start by blending tones. In practical papers, you may have a specimen: look twice, draw once, then look again to correct proportion before you label.',
      'Teachers sometimes display the best-looking book. Ask instead to see a script that scored full marks on a drawing. It is often plainer than you expect. Imitate that plainness. You are not being examined in art. You are being examined in whether a specialist can read a biological structure from your page.',
      'When a question says make a large drawing, obey. Large means it uses a sensible fraction of the space, not a stamp-sized sketch. When it says do not label, do not label. Command words apply to drawings too. Extra labels can introduce errors. Missing required labels are missing marks.',
      'If you cannot remember a name, still draw the part accurately and leave a guideline. A correct shape with a blank might pick up the drawing mark. A wrong name on a wrong shape loses both. Guessing a fancy term you half-remember is how a shoot label lands on a root.',
      'Before you leave the question, run a short visual check: outline clean, no shading, labels horizontal, lines not crossing, title present, number of required labels counted against the question. That check takes thirty seconds. It is the difference between a sketch you enjoyed making and a figure an examiner can mark without a debate.',
      'Take the same pencil into the hall that you used in practice. Surprise tools change line quality. Biology diagrams reward boring consistency: clear lines, true proportions, and names that point to the right place. The bean seed does not need to look edible. It needs to be readable.',
