      'The printed timetable put English Language on Monday, Government on Wednesday, and Literature on Friday, with a practical squeezed into the same stretch for a science classmate in the same house. The student had planned a calm one-subject-a-day revision month. The calendar did not agree. By Sunday night she was still trying to reread an entire Government textbook, and English had been left as I already speak it. Stacked papers punish that kind of leftover thinking. You need a switch plan, not a wish for a kinder timetable.',
      'A heavy week is a logistics problem. You cannot master three syllabuses from zero in seven days. You can protect sleep, keep energy even, and give each paper a last, specific job: the skill it actually tests. English wants timed writing and reading. Government wants structured points and examples. Literature wants texts you can quote thinly and discuss. Those jobs do not benefit from the same kind of night.',
      'Sleep is not a luxury you postpone until after results. A short night before a language paper reliably produces slower reading and weaker last paragraphs. Protect a regular lights-out as if it were a lesson on the timetable. If the household is noisy, agree a quiet window, use earplugs if you have them, and stop one more chapter an hour before you sleep. You are not being lazy. You are keeping the tool that sits all three papers: attention.',
      'Energy during the day needs food you actually have, water, and movement between sessions, not a heroic fast at the desk. Study in two or three blocks with a walk in the compound between them. A four-hour unbroken sit on Sunday looks like dedication and often produces the same page reread without memory. Switch subjects at the block boundary so Monday\'s paper does not steal Wednesday\'s last practice. A banana and a rest under a tree is a better bridge between papers than another unread chapter.',
      'Build a subject-switch plan on one sheet. For each of the three papers, write only the last useful task. English might get one timed essay and one comprehension. Government might get two past structured questions on the weakest topic. Literature might get character and theme cards for the text that appears first. Then assign those tasks to the days that are not exam mornings. Exam mornings get a light review, not a new topic.',
      'The day before a paper, stop collecting new notes. Run a short retrieval: close the book, write what you must not forget, check, stop. Pack materials that evening: cards, ruler, pencils, registration documents. Morning scrambling is how people forget a geometry set or a black pen. Lay out clothes and transport time. Logistics is part of exam technique when the week is crowded. If two papers fall on consecutive days, the evening between them is for the second paper only, not for rewriting the first in your head.',
      'Between two papers, do not hold a post-mortem that lasts the afternoon. A brief note of I left the summary too long can guide the next language paper if you have one. A two-hour reconstruction of every answer helps nobody and steals revision from the next subject. Eat, walk, then open only the next paper\'s last-task list.',
      'If a practical or an oral sits in the same week, treat it as a full paper in the plan, not an errand. Practicals need early nights too, and they need equipment checks the day before. Orals need voice practice, not silent reading. Putting them in the easy column is how they become the unexpected drain.',
      'Family and church or mosque commitments will not vanish. Tell people the exam days and the night before each one. Ask for noise to drop in that window. You cannot control every visitor, but you can stop volunteering for extra errands in that week. A stacked timetable is a reason to be boring on purpose.',
      'Do not invent a new colour-code system on the Sunday of a heavy week. Use the methods you already have. New systems are delay with stationery. If your Government notes are messy, do not recopy them; answer two questions from them. Production beats decoration when time is short.',
      'Ignore hallway rumours about what leaked and which paper was friendly. Those stories steal the hour you needed for retrieval and they are not a syllabus. If a classmate insists a topic is coming, thank them and return to your last-task list. The switch plan is dull on purpose so that panic has less room.',
      'If freeze arrives, shrink the task. Ten minutes of retrieval on one topic is better than staring at a full contents page. If you cannot start, write the heading of the last-task list and do the first five minutes. Starting is usually enough to break the stall. The week will not become gentle. It can still be orderly.',
      'After the third paper, rest before you evaluate your life. Tired brains write cruel stories about how the week went. Sleep, then return to ordinary revision for whatever remains. A stacked week is survived with switching and sleep, not with a legend about studying until dawn for all three.',
      'Walk into Monday with English already practised, Wednesday with Government points already spoken aloud, and Friday with Literature pages already closed and recalled. The timetable stacked the papers. Your plan unstacks the work so that each morning has one job.',
