      'Two classmates sat on a compound bench in Kumasi with a phone timer set to twenty minutes. They were not revising a novel. One read a short dialogue from an old oral test while the other listened for word stress and for the difference between long and short vowels. Then they swapped. Twenty minutes of that work taught them more than an evening of silently staring at a list of difficult words. WAEC Oral English is a performance paper. It rewards mouths and ears, not highlighters.',
      'The oral paper typically checks listening, speaking, and the sound system of English as the exam uses it: vowels, consonants, rhyme, stress, and dialogue. Your school will tell you the exact format for your year. The revision principle does not change. You must make the sounds, not only recognise them on a page.',
      'Start with sounds that your local language handles differently from exam English. Many candidates in the region need extra practice on the contrast between ship and sheep, bit and beat, and on consonants such as the two th sounds, v and b, or final consonants that get dropped in casual speech. Pick five pairs. Say them slowly, then in a short sentence. Record one round on a phone and listen once. Vanity is not the point. Hearing your own substitutions is.',
      'Word stress changes meaning and is a favourite test item. Practise two-syllable pairs where the stress shift matters, and longer school words: education, examination, photograph versus photography. Clap the strong syllable. If you and your partner disagree, check a dictionary that marks stress and say the word three times in a sentence, not in isolation only.',
      'Sentence stress and intonation belong in the same twenty minutes. Read a question and a reply from a dialogue: Are you going to the market? Not today. The rise on the question and the fall on the statement are part of sounding intelligible. Flat reading of every line is a habit from silent study. Break it on purpose.',
      'Dialogue practice should be tiny and repeated. Take a six-line conversation: greeting, request, clarification, answer, thanks, close. Perform it twice, swapping roles. Listen for dropped endings and for words that run together until they are unclear. The examiner needs to hear the word, not a blur that your friend can guess because they already know the script.',
      'Listening practice is the other half. If your paper includes a recorded passage or a teacher reading, you need to write while you hear. Have your partner read a short paragraph once, at speaking speed, while you answer two questions. Do not stop them. The skill is catching information in real time. Then swap.',
      'A twenty-minute pair session can follow a stable shape without becoming a ritual you copy forever. Five minutes of minimal pairs. Five minutes of stress clapping on a list of ten words. Five minutes of dialogue. Five minutes of listen-and-write. Next session, change the list and the dialogue. Variety in material, stability in time, is enough. If the timer ends mid-line, stop anyway; the point is a complete short rehearsal, not an endless correction loop.',
      'Do not try to pick up a new accent in a month. Intelligibility is the target: sounds that contrast, stress that lands, speech that a stranger could transcribe. Copying a film accent badly can hide your consonants. Clear West African exam English, spoken at a moderate pace, is a better goal than an imitation.',
      'If you have no partner some days, the phone timer still works. Read aloud. Record. Listen. Shadow one sentence from a news clip in English by repeating immediately after it. Solo work is second best, not useless. What does not work is reading a pronunciation list with your lips closed.',
      'In the test week, keep the voice used. Whispering all day then speaking once in the hall is a poor rehearsal. Sleep, water, and a short warm-up of reading a paragraph aloud in the morning are practical. Avoid shouting on the way to school; strained voices do not help consonants.',
      'Teachers can correct what you cannot hear. Ask for two minutes after class on the three sounds they always mark you down for. Bring your pair list. A precise correction on a th sound or on the stress in investigate is worth more than a general speak up. Then put that correction into the next twenty-minute session. If no teacher is free, a classmate who speaks a different home language is a useful listener, because they will not automatically fill in your missing consonants.',
      'On the day, sit so you can hear the source of the listening item, and speak to the examiner or the microphone, not into your chest. If you mishear, do not freeze for the rest of the paper. The next item is independent. Make the contrasts, land the stress, and keep a dialogue understandable. You can practise that on a bench. You cannot cram it from a silent page the night before.',
      'The pair on the bench did not become perfect speakers in one evening. They became people who had already performed the paper\'s jobs with a timer running. That is the advantage you want: not a memorised word list, but a mouth that has already done the work in daylight, with a classmate who will tell you when a vowel collapsed.',
